Yes, there are several webpack loaders that can transpile packages/common for you. Here are a few options:


 1. ts-loader: This is a popular TypeScript loader that can be used to transpile TypeScript files during the webpack build process. It supports both development and production modes, and can be used with both CommonJS and ES6 modules. To use ts-loader, you need to install it as a development dependency: npm install ts-loader -D. Then, in your webpack configuration, you can add a new loader for .ts and .tsx files, like this:


 module: {
  rules: [
  {
  test: /\.tsx?$/,
  use: [
  { loader: 'ts-loader', options: { transpileOnly: true } },
  ],
  },
  ],
 }


 2. tsc-loader: This is another TypeScript loader that can be used to transpile TypeScript files during the webpack build process. It works similarly to ts-loader, but has some additional features, such as support for type checking and type inference. To use tsc-loader, you need to install it as a development dependency: npm install tsc-loader -D. Then, in your webpack configuration, you can add a new loader for .ts and .tsx files, like this:


 module: {
  rules: [
  {
  test: /\.tsx?$/,
  use: [
  { loader: 'tsc-loader', options: { transpileOnly: true } },
  ],
  },
  ],
 }


 3. ts-node-dev: This is a development tool that can be used to transpile TypeScript files during development, and also provides hot module replacement and type checking. To use ts-node-dev, you need to install it as a development dependency: npm install ts-node-dev -D. Then, in your webpack configuration, you can add a new loader for .ts and .tsx files, like this:


 module: {
  rules: [
  {
  test: /\.tsx?$/,
  use: [
  { loader: 'ts-node-dev-loader', options: { transpileOnly: true } },
  ],
  },
  ],
 }


 4. react-hot-loader: This is a tool that can be used to enable hot module replacement for React components. It works by intercepting the render method of React components and replacing the rendered HTML with a new version, without having to reload the entire page. To use react-hot-loader, you need to install it as a development dependency: npm install react-hot-loader -D. Then, in your webpack configuration, you can add a new loader for .js and .jsx files, like this:


 module: {
  rules: [
  {
  test: /\.jsx?$/,
  use: [
  { loader: 'react-hot-loader/webpack', options: { transpileOnly: true } },
  { loader: 'babel-loader' },
  ],
  },
  ],
 }


 With these loaders, you can transpile packages/common during the webpack build process, and also enable hot module replacement for React components in your Next.js app.

Yes, you can use the ts-loader for that. In the Next.js app’s webpack.config.js file, you can add this to the rules array:


 {
 test: /\.tsx?$/,
 loader: 'ts-loader',
 include: [
 path.resolve(__dirname, '../common'),
 ],
 },


 Make sure to install the ts-loader module with npm install ts-loader -D and add the ts-loader to the resolve.extensions array as well.

